  heard: "a lane between wooden houses on stilts at night, crickets loud in the warm air; a few monks chant in unison as they walk, the women and men on the porches with lamps repeat the verse a little behind and not together, water drips from the sprinkling, a triangular kyi zi gong marks the ends",
  refs: [
    { title: "ရတနသုတ် Ratana Sutta (Myanmar recitation)", who: "Burmese monastic chant (Sarko Too)", url: "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5-JyKIm7h6w" },
    { title: "Ratana Sutta, Pali and Burmese", who: "Ashin Ariyadhamma", url: "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dzwYFmdosZM" },
  ],
  measured: "Burmese recitation of the Ratana Sutta (whole 8 min, mix): the reciting voice C3-A#3 (median 177 Hz) uses six pitches over a final about 20-25 cents above C# (written C#): 0, 190, 380, 530, 670, 870 cents, nearly even steps rather than Indian or Western intervals; verses fall to the final, the 530/670 pair carries the middle of each line; 2.6 syllables a second, no steady pulse (clarity 0.05), vibrato ~12 cents, 14% of notes glide. Ours: the same six pitches (the render gives 180, 370, 520, 660, 860 over C#, i.e. the measured steps but 20-25 cents low overall, since the key cannot take a cents offset), 2.5 syllables a second, the Pali in Burmese pronunciation (s as θ). Still different: our chant is still more metrical than the free recitation (pulse clarity 0.44 against 0.05) even with human=0.9, because notes fall on an eighth-note grid; the villagers' echo, the gong, crickets and water are the scene around it, not on the record.",
