  heard: "an evening wedding in the groom's house in Edo: lamps, a gold screen, the bride in white; the guests talk and the sake is poured, then a senior man of the family sits up straight and chants Takasago from the belly, slowly, on the three utai pitches; everyone falls silent until the last line, then the room breaks into 'omedetō gozaimasu'",
  refs: [
    { title: "Su-utai (solo) 'Takasago', Kanze school (能楽 観世流 素謡(独吟)「高砂」)", who: "Hayashi Kiemon XI, Kanze-school Noh actor (historic recording)", url: "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jfe798lgll4" },
  ],
  measured: "the solo utai (6.5 minutes): one man's voice centred on F (the chū-on), with a fourth above (Bb, jō-on, here 520 cents) and a fourth below (C, ge-on) as the main steps, the semitone below the centre (E, +1090 c) and a small step above (+140 c) used as ornaments; syllables mostly 0.2–0.9 s with phrase-final vowels held, vibrato ~7 cents, few glides (7 %), an old recording's narrow band (brightness 2.9 kHz, nothing under 250 Hz). Ours: scale 1 2(140) 4 5 7 on F, the travel song's text (Zeami's Takasago, public domain) one syllable a beat at 84 with the lines' ends held, the high opening 'Takasago ya' on the jō-on and each line falling to the chū-on or the ge-on as utai lines do; the melody follows the utai pitch system but is not a transcription of the Kanze notation (fushi); guests murmur before and cheer after. Rendered and measured (the chant alone): centre F with Bb the main other step, vibrato 8 cents against 7, about one onset a second; ours is darker (1.3 kHz against 2.9 kHz, but the reference is a narrow old recording) and smoother: the singer's syllables are separated by small pushes of breath that our legato voice runs together. Still differs: utai's strong belly-voice (tsuyogin colour) and its small pitch waverings (yuri) inside a held syllable",
